WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:13.310
You should treat skeptically the loud cries now coming from colleges and universities that the last bastion of excellence in American education is being gutted by state budget cuts and mounting costs.
如今，各高校大声疾呼，称州预算削减和不断攀升的成本正在掏空美国教育中最后一座卓越堡垒；对这种呼声，你应当持怀疑态度。

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:18.550
Whatever else it is, higher education is not a bastion of excellence.
无论高等教育还能算是什么，它都不是卓越的堡垒。

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:24.650
It is shot through with waste, lax academic standards and mediocre teaching and scholarship.
其中充斥着浪费、松弛的学术标准，以及平庸的教学和学术研究。

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:30.590
True, the economic pressures—from the Ivy League to state systems—are intense.
诚然，从常春藤盟校到州立大学系统，经济压力都很大。

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:37.410
Last year, nearly two-thirds of schools had to make midyear spending cuts to stay within their budgets.
去年，将近三分之二的学校不得不在年中削减开支，以免超出预算。

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:48.350
It is also true (as university presidents and deans argue) that relieving those pressures merely by raising tuitions and cutting courses will make matters worse.
正如大学校长和院长们所说，仅靠提高学费、削减课程来缓解这些压力，会使情况更糟，这一点同样不假。

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.980
Students will pay more and get less.
学生会花更多的钱，得到的却更少。

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.830
The university presidents and deans want to be spared from further government budget cuts.
大学校长和院长们希望能免遭政府进一步削减预算。

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:58.850
Their case is weak.
他们的理由站不住脚。

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.310
Higher education is a bloated enterprise.
高等教育是一个臃肿的行业。

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:07.290
Too many professors do too little teaching to too many ill-prepared students.
太多的教授教课太少，教的又是太多准备不足的学生。

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:12.810
Costs can be cut and quality improved without reducing the number of graduates.
不减少毕业生人数，也能削减成本、提高质量。

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.150
Many colleges and universities should shrink.
许多高校应当缩减规模。

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.490
Some should go out of business.
有些则应当停办。

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.320
Consider: Except for elite schools, admissions standards are low.
请看：除名校外，各校的录取标准都很低。

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:30.050
About 70 percent of freshmen at four-year colleges and universities attend their first-choice schools.
在四年制高校，约70%的新生上的是自己的第一志愿学校。

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.600
Roughly 20 percent go to their second choices.
约20%上的是第二志愿。

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.440
Most schools have eagerly boosted enrollments to maximize revenues (tuition and state subsidies).
大多数学校都急切地扩大了招生，以求收入（学费和州政府补贴）最大化。

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.620
Dropout rates are high.
辍学率很高。

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.690
Half or more of freshmen don’t get degrees.
半数或半数以上的新生拿不到学位。

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:56.440
A recent study of PhD programs at 10 major universities (including Harvard, Stanford and Yale) also found high dropout rates for doctoral candidates.
最近一项针对10所知名大学（包括哈佛、斯坦福和耶鲁）博士项目的研究也发现，博士生的辍学率很高。

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.680
The attrition among undergraduates is particularly surprising because college standards have apparently fallen.
本科生的流失尤其令人意外，因为大学的标准看来已经降低了。

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:12.040
One study of seven top schools (including Amherst, Duke and the University of Michigan) found widespread grade inflation.
一项针对七所顶尖学校（包括阿默斯特学院、杜克大学和密歇根大学）的研究发现，分数膨胀现象普遍存在。

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:19.080
In 1963, half of the students in introductory philosophy courses got a B- or worse.
1963年，哲学入门课程的学生中有一半得了B-或更低的成绩。

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.360
By 1986, only 21 percent did.
到1986年，这样的学生只占21%。

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.860
If elite schools have relaxed standards, the practice is almost surely widespread.
如果名校放松了标准，那么这种做法几乎可以肯定很普遍。

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.460
Faculty teaching loads have fallen steadily since the 1960s.
自20世纪60年代以来，教师的授课量一直在稳步下降。

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:40.280
In major universities, senior faculty members often do less than two hours a day of teaching.
在知名大学里，资深教师每天授课的时间往往不到两小时。

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:52.140
Professors are “socialized to publish, teach graduate students and spend as little time teaching [undergraduates] as possible,” concludes James Fairweather of Penn State University in a new study.
宾夕法尼亚州立大学的詹姆斯·费尔韦瑟在一项新研究中总结道：教授们“所受的熏陶就是要发表论著、教研究生，并尽可能少花时间教［本科生］”。

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:57.450
Faculty pay consistently rises as undergraduate teaching loads drop.
教师的薪酬总是随着本科授课量的减少而上涨。

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:03.130
Universities have encouraged an almost mindless explosion of graduate degrees.
各大学助长了研究生学位近乎盲目的激增。

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:12.810
Since 1960, the number of master’s degrees awarded annually has risen more than fourfold to 337,000.
自1960年以来，每年授予的硕士学位数量增长到原来的四倍多，达到337,000个。

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:25.770
Between 1965 and 1989, the annual number of MBAs (masters of business administration) jumped from 7,600 to 73,100.
1965年至1989年间，每年MBA（工商管理硕士）的数量从7,600猛增至73,100。

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.160
Even so, our system has strengths.
即便如此，我们的体制也有长处。

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:34.470
It boasts many top-notch schools and allows almost anyone to go to college.
它拥有许多一流学校，而且让几乎任何人都能上大学。

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.110
But mediocrity is pervasive.
但平庸现象无处不在。

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:43.030
We push as many freshmen as possible through the door, regardless of qualifications.
我们不问资质，把尽可能多的新生推进大学校门。

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:49.350
Because bachelor’s degrees are so common, we create more graduate degrees of dubious worth.
由于学士学位如此普遍，我们就设立更多价值可疑的研究生学位。

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:54.150
Does anyone believe the MBA explosion has improved management?
有谁相信MBA的激增提升了管理水平？

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.190
You won’t hear much about this from college deans or university presidents.
你很少会听到学院院长或大学校长谈起这些。

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.260
They created this mess and are its biggest beneficiaries.
他们造成了这个烂摊子，也是其中最大的受益者。

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.500
Large enrollments support large faculties.
大规模招生支撑着庞大的教师队伍。

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:15.960
More graduate students liberate tenured faculty from undergraduate teaching to concentrate on writing and research: the source of status.
研究生多了，终身教职教师就能摆脱本科教学，专心著述和研究，而这正是地位的来源。

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:27.690
Richard Huber, a former college dean, writes knowingly in a new book (“How Professors Play the Cat Guarding the Cream: Why We’re Paying More and Getting Less in Higher Education”):
曾任学院院长的理查德·休伯在新书《教授们如何扮演看守奶油的猫：为什么我们在高等教育上花得更多、得到的却更少》中以深谙内情的口吻写道：

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:35.240
“Presidents, deans and trustees … call for more recognition of good teaching with prizes and salary incentives.
“校长、院长和董事们……呼吁通过奖项和薪酬激励，让优秀的教学得到更多认可。

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:51.870
The reality is closer to the experience of Harvard University’s distinguished paleontologist Stephen Jay Gould: ‘To be perfectly honest, though lip service is given to teaching, I have never seriously heard teaching considered in any meeting for promotion….
实际情况却更接近哈佛大学杰出的古生物学家斯蒂芬·杰伊·古尔德的经历：‘说实话，虽然教学在口头上受到重视，但我从来没有在任何一次晋升会议上听到有人认真考虑过教学……

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.400
Writing is the currency of prestige and promotion.’”
著述是换取声望和晋升的通货。’”

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:03.450
About four-fifths of all students attend state-subsidized systems, from community colleges to prestige universities.
在全体学生中，约五分之四就读于由州政府补贴的院校系统，从社区学院到名牌大学都有。

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:09.300
How governors and state legislatures deal with their budget pressures will be decisive.
州长和州议会如何应对预算压力，将起决定性作用。

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:14.890
Private schools will, for better or worse, be influenced by state actions.
无论是好是坏，私立学校都会受到各州举措的影响。

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.660
The states need to do three things.
各州需要做三件事。

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.040
First, create genuine entrance requirements.
第一，设立真正的入学要求。

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.830
Today’s low standards tell high school students: You don’t have to work hard to go to college.
如今的低标准等于在告诉高中生：你不必努力学习就能上大学。

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:35.510
States should change the message by raising tuitions sharply and coupling the increase with generous scholarships based on merit and income.
各州应当改变这一信号，办法是大幅提高学费，同时辅以根据成绩和收入发放的丰厚奖学金。

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.900
To get scholarships, students would have to pass meaningful entrance exams.
学生要获得奖学金，就必须通过有实质意义的入学考试。

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.120
Ideally, the scholarships should be available for use at in-state private schools.
理想的情况是，这些奖学金应当能在本州的私立学校使用。

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:51.840
All schools would then compete for students on the basis of academic quality and costs.
这样一来，所有学校就会凭学术质量和费用来争夺生源。

00:05:52.640 --> 00:06:01.220
Today’s system of general tuition subsidies provides aid to well-to-do families that don’t need it or to unqualified students who don’t deserve it.
如今普遍补贴学费的制度，把资助给了并不需要资助的富裕家庭，或是给了不配得到资助的不合格学生。

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:06.480
Next, states should raise faculty teaching loads, mainly at four-year schools.
其次，各州应当提高教师的授课量，主要是在四年制学校。

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.250
(Teaching loads at community colleges are already high.)
（社区学院的授课量已经很高了。）

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:15.170
This would cut costs and reemphasize the primacy of teaching at most schools.
这样做会削减成本，并在大多数学校重新突显教学的首要地位。

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:21.010
What we need are teachers who know their fields and can communicate enthusiasm to students.
我们需要的是熟悉本专业领域、能把热情传递给学生的教师。

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.410
Not all professors can be path-breaking scholars.
并非所有教授都能成为开拓性的学者。

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:32.120
The excessive emphasis on scholarship generates many unread books and mediocre articles in academic journals.
过分强调学术研究，催生了许多无人阅读的书和学术期刊上的平庸文章。

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:38.120
“You can’t do more of one (research) without less of the other (teaching),” says Fairweather.
费尔韦瑟说：“多做一样（研究），就必然少做另一样（教学）。”

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.920
“People are working hard—it’s just where they’re working.”
“大家都在努力工作——问题只在于他们在哪方面下功夫。”

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.350
Finally, states should reduce or eliminate the least useful graduate programs.
最后，各州应当削减或取消用处最小的研究生项目。

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:53.460
Journalism (now dubbed “communications”), business and education are prime candidates.
新闻学（如今改称“传播学”）、商科和教育学是首要对象。

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.370
A lot of what they teach can—and should—be learned on the job.
它们所教的很多内容，都可以而且应该在工作中学到。

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:06.310
If colleges and universities did a better job of teaching undergraduates, there would be less need for graduate degrees.
如果高校在本科生教学上做得更好，对研究生学位的需求就会减少。

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:12.300
Our colleges and universities need to provide a better education to deserving students.
我们的高校需要为够格的学生提供更好的教育。

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:19.500
This may mean smaller enrollments, but given today’s attrition rates, the number of graduates need not drop.
这可能意味着招生规模缩小，但考虑到当前的流失率，毕业生人数未必会下降。

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:24.940
Higher education could become a bastion of excellence, if we would only try.
只要我们肯努力，高等教育就可能成为卓越的堡垒。
